\chapter{Strings and error interpolation}
A string is an immutable sequence of bytes. Its literal form uses double
quotes and supports escapes for newline, carriage return, tab, NUL, backslash,
and double quote. Unknown escape sequences are rejected rather than silently
losing the backslash.

\begin{mullcode}
val greeting <- "Hello, " + "MuLL";
G:Assert(greeting == "Hello, MuLL");
G:Assert(G:Len("a\0b") == 3);
G:Assert("a\0b"[1] == "\0");
G:Assert("quote: \"" == "quote: " + "\"");
\end{mullcode}

\section{Byte indexing}
Indexing returns a one-byte string, and \api{G:Len} counts bytes. Thus a
UTF-8 character may occupy several indexes. The text library's case conversion
is deliberately ASCII-only; it leaves other bytes unchanged. Applications
needing Unicode segmentation can register a dedicated native library.

\section{The import error marker}
Every occurrence of \api{$E?} in a string literal is replaced by the runtime's
current import error when that literal is evaluated. Replacement is one pass:
the inserted error text is not recursively interpreted. Successful imports,
including cache hits, clear the error text. Other runtime errors throw directly
and do not update this import-error slot.

This is the only built-in string interpolation. To format arbitrary values,
use \api{Printf:Format} with brace placeholders. To construct the literal
marker itself without interpolation, concatenate \api{"$E"} and \api{"?"}.
The resulting runtime string is not rescanned as source code.
